# day 1 · piece 3 of 3 — answers.txt: three keys you will use every day from now on
#
# Three lines, one answer each, in this order. Write keys the way `bind -p` does
# (M-. or \e. or Alt-. are all accepted; C-x C-e or Ctrl-x Ctrl-e likewise).
#   Q1  the readline KEY that inserts the LAST ARGUMENT of the previous command
#   Q2  the HISTORY designator (the `!` form) that means the same thing
#   Q3  the readline KEY that opens the current line in $EDITOR
# Try them in a real shell first: `ls /etc`, then `cat ` and press the Q1 key.
# The #RUN shows the file with `cat -A`, so you can see every line end ($) and stray tab.
#DAY 1
#FILE answers.txt
#RUN cat -A answers.txt
# replace these three lines with your three answers, in order
